# {{title}}

A DjangoBaseX plugin: one Django app (`{{package}}`) and, later, its Next.js half (`frontend/`).
It lives in its own repository and is mounted into a product as a git submodule under
`backend/plugins/{{name}}/`.

## What it owns

Every name it creates is under its label, `{{label}}`, so it cannot collide with the platform or
another plugin (`CORE_ARCHITECTURE_PLAN.md` § 3). `plugin.toml` declares them, and discovery
refuses a manifest whose prefixes disagree with the label:

| Thing | Prefix | Example |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Database tables | `{{label}}_` | `{{label}}_items` (set `Meta.db_table` explicitly) |
| Permissions | `{{label}}.` | `{{label}}.home.view` |
| API routes | `/api/{{route}}/` | `/api/{{route}}/` (URL names `{{label}}:`) |
| Frontend routes | `/{{route}}` | `/{{route}}` |

## The rules

- **Import only Django, third-party packages and `core.*`.** Never another plugin, never `project`.
  The platform knows this plugin only through what `wiring.py` registers.
- **The app label is explicit** in `apps.py`. Never remove it: `app_label` is written into every
  migration.
- **Layers** (`AGENTS.md` § 3): thin views, logic in `services.py`, no queries in serializers.
- **Every view declares `required_permissions`**, and every code it uses is declared in
  `wiring.py`.

## Mounting it

From the product's `backend/` directory:

```bash
uv run python ../scripts/plugins.py link {{name}}      # the frontend half, once it exists
uv run python manage.py makemigrations {{label}}
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python ../scripts/plugins.py list               # confirm it is discovered and linked
```

Discovery is automatic: `config/settings.py` installs every plugin under `backend/plugins/`.
